# Context

## Background
The owner read the review of 2026-11-25 and asked to fix every open
item. One was the store transaction that never waited for the write
lock. The ingest journal had 1,508 "database is locked" lines in ten
days, 1,494 of them in one hour.

## Why This Way
BEGIN IMMEDIATE is the remedy that the SQLite documentation names for
a busy error in the middle of a transaction, and the registry database
of this repository already uses it. The alternative, to retry a
deferred transaction in the caller, would keep the race and add code
at nine sites. PlanPurge moved to a reader pool in the same change,
because an immediate transaction would hold the write lock for the
whole plan.

## Gotchas
- A deferred transaction that fails at once costs no time. An
  immediate transaction waits, so a read that shares the single writer
  connection waits with it. The hub answers remote SQL through its
  reader pool, so queries stay fast.
- A canceled context does not end the wait. The driver calls
  sqlite3_interrupt, but sqliteDefaultBusyCallback of SQLite 3.54.1
  never reads the interrupt flag. A service that stops while it waits
  for the lock can take 5 s longer.
- The time of a test package on this VM swings with the disk load:
  internal/store took 10 s and 49 s on the same commit. Compare two
  trees in the same round before a timing difference is called a
  regression.

